G5192.d:
ὑακίνθος, -ου, ό 
 [in LXX chiefly for תְּכֵלֶת ;] 
hyacinth; 
  (a) in cl., a flower, prob. the dark blue iris; 
  (b) in late writers, a precious stone of the same colour, perhaps the sapphire: Rev.21:20 (Phil., FlJ, al.).†
 (AS)

G5207.d:
υἱός, -οῦ, ὁ, [in LXX very frequently and nearly always for בֵּן, Gen.4:17, al.; for בַּר, Dan LXX TH 7:13, al.; etc. ;], 
 a son; 
  1. in the ordinary sense: Matt.10:37, Mark.9:17, Luke.1:13, al. mult.; omitted with the art. of origin (WM, §30, 3; Bl., §35, 2), τὸν τοῦ Ἰεσσαί, Acts.13:22 (LXX); also with genitive anarth. (cl.), Σώπατρος Πύρρου Βεροιαῖος, Acts.20:4; with adj., προτότοκος, Luke.2:7; μονογένης, Luke.7:12; opposite to νόθος, Heb.12:8; in a wider sense, of posterity: ὁ υἱ. Δαυΐδ, of the Messiah (cf. Dalman, Words, 316ff.; DCG, ii, 653f.), Matt.22:42, 45 Mark.12:35, 37 Luke.20:41, 44 al.; υἱοὶ  Ἰσραήλ, (cf. υἷες Ἀχαιῶν, Hom., Il., i, 162, al.), Matt.27:9, Acts.9:15, al. 
  2. Metaphorical; 
  (a) as belonging to, being connected with or having the quality of that which follows (a usage mainly due to translation from a Semitic original; cf. Deiss., BS, 161ff.; Dalman, Words, 115f.; DCG, ii, 652f.): τ. πονεροῦ (διαβόλου), Matt.13:38, Acts.13:10; τ. νυμφῶνος (see: νυμφών), Matt.9:15, Mark.2:19, al.; τ. φωτός (Lft., Notes, 74), Luke.16:8, John.12:36, 1 Thess.5:5; τ. εἰρεήμης, Luke.10:6; γεέννης, Matt.23:15; τ. ἀπωλείας, John.17:12, 2 Thess.2:3; τ. αἰῶνος τούτου, Luke.16:8 20:34; τ. ἀπειθειάς, Eph.2:2 5:6; βροντῆς, Mark.3:17; τ. ἀναστάσεως, Luke.20:36; παρακλήσεως, Acts.4:36; τ. προφητῶν κ. τ. διαθήκης, Acts.3:25; 
  (b) υἱὸς τ. θεοῦ (cf. Dalman, Words, 268ff.; Deiss., BS, 166f.; DB, iv, 570 ff.; DCG, ii, 654ff.), of men, as partakers of the Divine nature and of the life to come: Matt.5:9, Luke.20:36, Rom.8:14 9:26, al.; υἱοὶ (κ. θυγατέρες) τ. ὑψίστου, Luke.6:35, 2 Cor.6:18; in an unique sense of Jesus, Matt.4:3 8:29 28:19, Mark.3:4, Luke.4:41, John.9:35 11:27, al.; ὁ Χριστὸς ὁ υἱ. τ. θεοῦ ζῶντος (τ. εὐλογητοῦ), Matt.16:16, Mark.14:61; 
  (with) (ὁ) υἱὸς τοῦ ἀνθρώπου (in LXX for Heb. בּן אדם, Aram, בּר אנשׁ; cf. Dalman, Words, 234ff.; DB, iv, 579ff.; DCG, ii, 659ff.; Westc., St. John, i, 74ff.; other reff. in Swete, Mk, 2:10), based on the Aram. of Dan.7:13, where the phrase, like the corresponding Heb. (as in Ps.8:5), means a man, one of the species, and indicates the human appearance of the person in question. It is used of the Messiah in Enoch, with 46, §1-4, also in II Est.13:3, 12, al. Our Lord first makes the phrase a title, using the def. art. It seems to combine the ideas of his true humanity and representative character. Exc. in Acts.7:56 and (anarth.) Rev.1:13 14:14, it is used of Jesus only by himself: Matt.8:20, Mark.2:10, Luke.5:24, John.1:52, al. 
 (AS)

G5215.d:
ὕμνος, -ου, ὁ 
 [in LXX for תְּהִלָּה, נְגִינָה, שִׁיר, etc. ;] 
a hymn; 
  (a) in cl. a festal song in praise of gods or heroes; 
  (b) in LXX and NT a song of praise addressed to God: Eph.5:19, Col.3:16.†
  SYN.: ψαλμός, that which is sung to a musical accompaniment; ᾠδή, the generic term for song (cf. Tr., Syn., § LXXviii, Lft. on Col.3:16)  (AS)

G5244.d:
ὑπερήφανος, -ον
 (ὑπέρ, φαίνομαι, with η pleonast., see Kühner3, I, 189) [in LXX for זֵד, גֵּאֶה, לוּץ, etc. ;] 
showing oneself above others; 
  (a) in good sense (Plat., al.), pre-eminent, splendid; 
  (b) more frequently in had sense, and so always in Scr., arrogant, haughty, disdainful (see Westc.  Epp. Jo., 65b): Rom.1:30, 2 Tim.3:2; διανοίᾳ καρδίας, Luke.1:51; opposite to ταπεινός (as in Pro.3:34), Jas.4:6, 1 Pet.5:5" (LXX) .†
 SYN.: ἀλαζών, ὑβριστής, see Tr., Syn., § xxix  (AS)

G5265.d:
ὑπο-δέω 
 [in LXX: 2Ch.28:15, Eze.16:10 (נָעַל) * ;] 
to blind under, esp. of foot gear. Most frequently in mid, and pass. with accusative; 
  (a) of the foot, ὑποδησάμενοι τ. πόδας, your feet shod: Eph.6:15; 
  (b) of that which is put on, σανδάλια: Mark.6:9, Acts.12:8.†
 (AS)

G5438.d:
φυλακή, -ῆς, ἡ
 (φυλάσσω) [in LXX chiefly for מִשְׁמֶרֶת and  cogn. forms, also for כֶּלֶא etc. ;] 
  (a) actively, a guarding, guard, watch (Hom., Plat., Xen., al.): cogn. accusative, φυλάσσειν φυλακάς, to keep watch, Luke.2:8; 
  (b) of those who keep watch (as also Lat. custodia), a guard; pl., sentinels, a guard (Hom., al.): Acts.12:10; 
  (with) of the place where persons are kept under guard, a prison (Hdt., Thuc., al.): Matt.14:10, Mark.6:17, Luke.3:20, Acts.5:19, 2 Cor.6:5, 1 Pet.3:19, Rev.18:2, al.; 
  (d) of the time during which guard was kept by night (Lest. vigilia; Anthol.), a watch: Matt.14:25 24:43, Mark.6:48, Luke.12:38 
 (AS)

G5442.d:
φυλάσσω 
 [in LXX chiefly and very frequently for שָׁמַר, also for נָצַר, etc. ;] 
to guard, watch; 
  (a) to guard or watch: with cogn. accusative, φυλακάς, Luke.2:8; with accusative of person(s), Acts.12:4 28:16; pass., Luke.8:29, Acts.23:35; with accusative of thing(s), Acts.22:20; 
  (b) to guard or protect: with accusative, Luke.11:21, John.12:25 17:12, 2 Thess.3:3, 1 Tim.6:20, 2 Tim.1:12 1:14, 2 Pet.2:5; ἑαυτὸν ἀπό, 1 John.5:21 (Westc., in l); metaphorically of law, precept, etc., to keep, preserve, observe: Matt.19:20, Luke.11:28 18:21, John.12:47, Acts.7:53 16:4 21:24, Rom.2:26, Gal.6:13, 1 Tim.5:21. Mid., to be on one's guard (against), keep oneself from, beware of: with accusative, Acts.21:25, 2 Tim.4:15; before ἀπό., Luke.12:15; ἵνα μή, 2 Pet.3:17; as in LXX (Exod.12:17, Lev.18:4, al.), of laws, etc., to keep, observe: ταῦτα πάντα, Mark.10:20 (cf. δια-φυάσσω).†
 SYN.: τηρέω, q.v  (AS)

G5449.d:
φύσις, -εως, ἡ
 (φύω) [in LXX: Wis.7:20 13:1 19:20 אΑ, 3Ma.3:29, 4Ma.1:20 4Mac 5:7-8 4Mac 5:25 4Mac 13:27 4Mac 15:13 4Mac 15:25 4Mac 16:3 * ;] 
nature, i.e., 
  (a) the nature (natural powers or constitution) of a person or thing: Jas.3:7, 2 Pet.1:4; 
 τέκνα φυσει ὀργῆς, Eph.2:3; 
  (b) origin, birth (Soph., Xen., al.): Rom.2:27, Gal.2:15; 
  (with) nature, i.e. the regular order or law of nature: 1 Cor.11:14; dative, φύσει adverbially, by nature, Rom.2:14, Gal.4:8; παρὰ φύσιν, against nature, Rom.1:26 11:24; καρὰ φ., according to nature, naturally, Rom.11:21, 24.†
 (AS)

G5456.d:
φωνή, -ῆς, ἡ, 
 [in LXX chiefly and very frequently for קוֹל ;] 
a voice; 
  (a) prop., of persons, Matt.2:18 (LXX), al.; φ. αἴρειν (ἐπαίρειν), Luke.17:13, Acts.2:14, al.; φ. μεγάλῃ εἰπεῖν (λέγειν, φωνεῖν, etc.), Luke.8:28, Acts.7:57, Rev.5:12, al.; γίνεται (ἔρχεται) φ. ἐκ τ. οὐρανῶν (ἐξ οὐρανοῦ), Mark.1:11, Luke.3:22, John.12:28, al. (cf. DCG, ii, 810a; Dalman, Words, 204f.); ἀκούειν φωνήν (-ῆς; see: ἀκούω), Acts.9:4, 7 al.; φ. βοῶντος, Matt.3:3, Mark.1:3, Luke.3:4, John.1:23" (LXX) ; τ. θεοῦ, John.5:37, Heb.3:7, al. By meton., 
  (α) of the speaker, βλέπειν τὴν φ., Rev.1:12; 
  (β) speech, language ( Gen.11:1, 4Ma.12:7, al.): 1 Cor.14:10; 
  (b) of inanimate things: Matt.24:31, John.3:8, Acts.2:6, Rev.1:15b 9:9 14:2, al. (cf. Tr., Syn., §lxxxix). 
 (AS)

G5457.d:
φῶς (Att.. contr. from φάος; φάω), genitive, φωτός, τό, 
 [in LXX chiefly for אוֹר ;] 
light (opposite to τὸ σκότος, ἡ σκοτία): Matt.17:2, 5, John.11:9-10, 2 Cor.4:6; ἡλίου, Rev.22:5; of a lamp, Luke.8:16 11:33, John.5:35, Rev.18:23; of a  supernatural heavenly light, Acts.9:3 12:7 22:6, 9 22:11 26:13; hence, ἄγγελος φωτός, 2 Cor.11:14; ὁ κλήρος τ. ἁγίων ἐν τ. φ., Col.1:12; of the divine glory, Rev.21:24; by meton., of that which gives light: of fire, Mark.14:54, Luke.22:56 (cl.; 1Ma.12:29; cf. DCG, i, 595; pl., of a lamp or torch, Acts.16:29; of heavenly bodies, Jas.1:17. 
 Metaphorical, 
  (a) of God: 1 John.1:5 Jn 1:7; φῶς οἰκῶν ἀπρόσιτον, 1 Tim.6:16; 
  (b) of spiritual truth and its effects on the lives of men: Matt.4:16 5:16, John.1:4-5 3:19-21, Acts.26:18, 23, 2 Cor.6:14, Eph.5:13, 1 Pet.2:9, 1 John.2:8; τ. φ. τ. ζωῆς, John.8:12; τ. ὅπλα τοῦ φ., Rom.13:12; καρπὸς τοῦ φ., Eph.5:9; έν τ. φ. περιπατεῖν (εἶναι, μένειν), 1 John.1:7 Jn 2:9-10; υἱοὶ (τέκνα) τοῦ φ., Luke.16:8, John.12:36, Eph.5:8, 1 Thess.5:5; by meton., of one from whom truth shines forth: Acts.13:47 (LXX), Rom.2:19; esp. of Christ, Luke.2:32, John.1:7-8 12:35-36, 46; τ. φ. τ. κόσμου, John.8:12 9:5;τ. φ. τ. ἀληθινόν, John.1:9; of Christians, Matt.5:14, Eph.5:8; 
  (with) of the spiritual under­standing: τ. φ. τὸ ἐν σοί, Matt.6:23, Luke.11:35; 
  (d) adverbially, of that which is open to view (opposite to ἐν τ. σκοτίᾳ): ἐν τ. φ., Matt.10:27, Luke.12:3.†
 SYN.: see: φέγγος  (AS)

G5465.d:
χαλάω, -ῶ 
 [in LXX: (Jer.38:6) (שָׁלַח pi.), etc. ;] 
  (a) to slacken, loosen; 
  (b) to let loose, let go; 
  (with) to lower, let down: with accusative of thing(s), Mark.2:4, Luke.5:4-5, Acts.9:25 27:17, 30; with accusative of person(s) (cf. Je, l.with), pass., 2 Cor.11:33.†
 (AS)

G5467.d:
χαλεπός, -ή, -όν 
 [in LXX: Isa.18:2 (יָרֵא), Wis.3:19, Sir.3:21, al. ;] 
hard; 
  (a) hard to do or deal with, difficult; 
  (b) hard to bear, painful, grievous: καιροί, 2 Tim.3:1; 
  (with) of persons, hard to deal with, harsh, fierce, savage: Matt.8:28.†
 (AS)
